From 1062b9e3afc68d77a0ca5ecad2250fb23f2b2759 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 05:49:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] krans: a chunk under 256 bytes has one context;
 one-context chunks skip the context tables

A valid leaf could be made of one-byte coded chunks that each declare thousands of contexts: the
decoder then built the context tables and the context map for every output byte. Measured with
results/R02-work/final/tinychunks.c (200,000 one-byte chunks of 3,696 contexts, 3.2 MB of input,
this container, indicative): 5.81 us per chunk at 89d62b8e. The format now refuses more than one
context in a chunk of fewer than 256 bytes (the encoder never wrote one below 2048 unless forced
by a test; a forced set on a shorter chunk is stored), and a one-context chunk no longer fills
the tables nobody reads. No stream written by the encoder's own choices changes.

tests/test_krans.c: 500 one-byte coded chunks decode; the same chunk with contexts is refused.
SPEC.md and krans.h state the rule.

Review R02, claim ctx-rans-leaf (#11).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FUEN4aRWhUw7yL7cZMqTmZ
